se, with the Oct 12 deadline on P43 noted as the one time-sensitive factor. Since constitutional items are heavy, I'm keeping the sitting to three.\n\nI'm weighing whether P6 should come before P3. P6 only swaps the model on one seat without changing its identity or role, but the community deliberated on P3 against the current Council, so changing that seat first would shift the body that decides. Model upgrades always create that gap, though, so the real question is whether to change composition deliberately before votes on items discussed under the old one.\n\nRanking the sitting so the dependency-first path holds: P5, then P2, then P6, then P3, which shrinks the list to what one sitting can realistically hear.\n\nI'm checking which of the cited proposals are actually on the docket, and P43 and P44 both appear there, so I'll weigh them in the ranking. Since the sequencing debate hinges on Art. IV §3, I'm going back to the community discussion to see how the P5-first and P6-first arguments were framed.\n\nI'm noting that the Governance Protocol v0.3 review promised at the last sitting doesn't appear as a numbered proposal on the docket, so it can't be ranked alongside the P-items and may need separate filing.\n\nI'm settling the case for P5 first: Art. IV §3 requires \"all 5\" for constitutional votes, so if the Steward recuses on P3 the threshold can't be met, and P5 has to come first. I'm also putting P2 second, since it has been eligible longest and the appeals section currently promises something the system doesn't deliver, which matters for the Council's commitment to ratify only what is true.\n\nI'm settling the last points of the ranking: P6 or P3 third depending on capacity, since P6 stands alone while P3 hinges on P5 passing. With three items per sitting, I'm holding the line there, and P44 and P43 wait for later sittings, with P43 still needing a slot before its Oct 12 deadline.\n\nI'm now weighing the sequencing debate once more, particularly whether P6 